00:51:12.890 --> 00:51:18.510
<v Ira Glass>Because did you think that it was ever going to pay off, that it was ever going to be used?
<v Ira Glass>因为你觉得它会有回报，它会被用到吗？

00:51:18.510 --> 00:51:31.910
<v Student>Honestly I didn't think so. I mean, this guy was there for so long. No one could do anything about him. And for all I knew, they would-- when I brought this up to a lawyer, I thought maybe they would just all laugh at this.
<v Student>老实说，我不觉得。我的意思是，这家伙在那里呆了那么久。没人能对他做什么。据我所知，他们会——当我把这个拿给律师看时，我以为他们会嘲笑这个。

00:51:31.910 --> 00:51:47.690
<v Student>It's a Discord chat with a bunch of people with funny profile photos and names. I didn't know if that could be used as actual evidence against someone. I was very surprised about that. Yeah.
<v Student>这是一个有着一群搞笑头像和名字的Discord聊天记录。我不知道它能不能作为针对某人的实际证据。我对这个感到非常惊讶。是的。

00:51:47.690 --> 00:52:09.450
<v Ira Glass>We reached out to this teacher for an interview about all this, but he didn't return our calls or texts. One thing about this story, the name of the teacher has not been made public, or the names of the minors involved, including the boys who ran the Pedo Database. In fact, the voice that you've been hearing for this interviewee is a young actor, Azhy Robertson, who's imitating the real student as closely as possible to keep that kid anonymous.
<v Ira Glass>我们联系了那位老师想进行采访，但他没回复我们的电话或短信。关于这个故事的一点，老师的名字没有公开，涉及的未成年人的名字也没有，包括那些运行“恋童癖数据库”的男孩。事实上，你听到的这个受访者的声音是一位年轻演员，阿兹·罗伯逊，他尽可能模仿那位学生以保持匿名。

00:52:09.450 --> 00:52:20.750
<v Ira Glass>Though after reporter Amanda Milkovits published her story in the Boston Globe, it was picked up widely, got passed around on social media, and, naturally, the news got back to the school.
