00:52:20.750 --> 00:52:34.850
<v Student>We had seen some people reposting it on Instagram. I think I heard a couple of people talking about-- my friend told me that his-- a lot of people in his math class were talking about it. This is a friend that was part of the log with me.
<v Student>我们看到一些人在Instagram上转发它。我想我听到几个人在谈论——我朋友告诉我他的——他数学课上的很多人都在谈论它。这是跟我一起记录的朋友。

00:52:34.850 --> 00:52:38.960
<v Ira Glass>Wait a minute. And so people were talking about it at school. Do they know it's you?
<v Ira Glass>等一下。所以人们在学校里讨论它。他们知道是你吗？

00:52:38.960 --> 00:52:47.950
<v Student>Not the ones that weren't involved in the log. Because obviously staying anonymous to protect the integrity of the case is a big-- is important.
<v Student>那些没参与记录的人不知道。因为很明显，保持匿名来保护案件的完整性非常重要。

00:52:47.950 --> 00:52:52.380
<v Ira Glass>And so what's it like for you? You're like Clark Kent going around. You can't tell anybody you're Superman.
<v Ira Glass>那对你来说是什么感觉？你就像克拉克·肯特四处走动。你不能告诉任何人你是超人。

00:52:52.380 --> 00:53:01.860
<v Student>[LAUGHS] Yeah, somewhat. I mean, I'm not really-- I didn't do this for fame. I'm not looking for that. I mean, it's cool that people know what we did, but--
<v Student>[笑] 是的，有点。我的意思是，我不是真的——我做这个不是为了名声。我不是在寻找那个。我的意思是，人们知道我们做了什么很酷，但——

00:53:01.860 --> 00:53:11.010
<v Ira Glass>What are you talking about? I'm picturing you in the lunchroom, and people are talking about what you guys did. And then you have to keep your mouth shut and be like-- you can't say like, oh, that's me.
<v Ira Glass>你在说什么？我脑海里浮现出你在食堂，人们在谈论你们做的事情。然后你不得不闭上嘴，表现得——你不能说，噢，那是我。

00:53:11.010 --> 00:53:20.010
<v Student>I don't overhear a lot of the background chatter, but some of my friends do. And I can imagine for them it might be like, I don't know, hard to keep their mouth shut.
<v Student>我没听到太多背景闲聊，但我的一些朋友听到了。我可以想象对他们来说，很难闭嘴。

00:53:20.010 --> 00:53:21.990
<v Ira Glass>And it's not hard for you to keep your mouth shut?
<v Ira Glass>对你来说很难闭嘴吗？

00:53:21.990 --> 00:53:26.960
<v Student>Not at all, no.
<v Student>一点也不，不。

00:53:26.960 --> 00:53:51.230
<v Ira Glass>After the teacher got put on leave and the Pedo Database made the news, a young woman, an adult, came forward and said that she'd also been harassed by the same teacher years ago. She wrote a letter to the Pedo Database boys. She said, "when we're young, we're told that adults know best, that they know everything. Now as an adult, the only thing I know is that stupid kids just turn into even stupider adults.
<v Ira Glass>在老师被停职和“恋童癖数据库”上新闻后，一位年轻女性，一个成年人，站出来说她多年前也曾被同一个老师骚扰过。她给“恋童癖数据库”男孩们写了一封信。她说：“当我们年轻时，被告知成年人最了解，他们知道一切。现在作为成年人，我知道的唯一一件事就是愚蠢的孩子只会变成更愚蠢的成年人。”

00:53:51.230 --> 00:54:04.830
<v Ira Glass>When I was a student in this teacher's class, I felt helpless against his harassment and bullying. None of the other adults took my feelings seriously, not to mention the blind eye that teachers would turn to the creepy relationships that he had with other students.
<v Ira Glass>当我是这位老师班上的学生时，我在他的骚扰和欺凌面前感到无助。没有其他成年人认真对待我的感受，更不用说老师对他在与其他学生之间的诡异关系所表现出的视而不见。

00:54:04.830 --> 00:54:24.030
<v Ira Glass>Hearing that the same thing still happening in his class nearly seven years after I left leaves me feeling sad and frustrated with the school system's lack of action. Thank you so much for standing up for all the students who didn't have a voice. You're very brave for taking a stand against something you knew was wrong. And you should be very proud of yourselves for that. Thank you."
<v Ira Glass>听说在我离开近七年后同样的事情仍然在他的班上发生，让我对学校系统缺乏行动感到悲伤和沮丧。非常感谢你们为所有没有声音的学生挺身而出。你们为站出来反对一件你们明知是错误的事情而非常勇敢。你们应该为此感到非常自豪。谢谢。

00:54:24.030 --> 00:54:30.800
<v Ira Glass>The boy told me that it felt good to read that. It made it feel like a bigger deal, what they'd done.
<v Ira Glass>那个男孩告诉我，读到那个感觉很好。这让事情感觉像是一个大事件，他们所做的事情。

00:54:30.800 --> 00:54:51.890
<v Ira Glass>Years ago, I was friends with a political reporter at the Rand Daily Mail, the big opposition newspaper in South Africa back during the height of apartheid. They published story after story about terrible things the white government was doing back then. Over decades, it had little or no effect on the government at all. The brutality in bantustans and suppressions of dissent continued.
<v Ira Glass>多年前，我和《兰德每日邮报》的一位政治记者是朋友，那是种族隔离时期南非的大型反对派报纸。他们发表了一篇又一篇关于当时白人政府所做可怕事情的故事。几十年来，它对政府几乎没有影响。班图斯坦的暴行和对异议的镇压仍在继续。

00:54:51.890 --> 00:55:16.290
<v Ira Glass>He and I talked once about what the point of publishing was in that setting. Said that even if it didn't seem to be doing much good right then, he and his editors felt that it was important that there was a record, that someone noted what was happening and wrote it down, so that later nobody could deny it happened.
<v Ira Glass>我和他曾经谈过在这种情况下发表新闻的意义。他说即使当时看起来没产生多大效果，他和他的编辑们认为重要的是存在一个记录，有人注意到发生了什么并写了下来，以便后来没有人可以否认它发生过。

00:55:16.290 --> 00:55:31.850
<v Ira Glass>Can I say, OK, when I finished writing this story yesterday, that's where I ended this story. Because it seemed like a good way to capture what these middle school boys had done. But then, today on my way to work, I thought about the Stop the Stealers in Act 1 of today's show.
<v Ira Glass>我可以这样说，好吧，当我昨天写完这个故事时，我就是在那里结束的。因为它似乎是捕捉这些初中男孩所做之事的最好方式。但今天在上班路上，我想到了Act 1节目里的“停止窃选者”。

00:55:31.850 --> 00:56:25.200
<v Ira Glass>And I realized how that idea, the idea that if you make a factual record of what happened, a truthful record, that in the future nobody can deny it. That idea feels like it's from just another era. Things are so different today. The Stop the Stealers continue to believe a whole set of lies about the 2020 election being stolen, and they are trying to remake the world based on denying what really happened. And I know that is not an especially uplifting note to end the show on today, but I think in dark times, it does no good to pretend that you're not living in dark times.
<v Ira Glass>我意识到那个想法，如果你对发生了什么做一个事实记录，一个真实的记录，那么在未来没有人可以否认。那个想法感觉就像是来自另一个时代的。今天一切都太不同了。停止窃选者继续相信关于2020年选举被窃取的一整套谎言，他们正试图基于否认真正发生过的事情来重塑世界。我知道这不是今天节目结束时特别令人振奋的音符，但我认为在黑暗时代，假装你并没有生活在黑暗时代是毫无意义的。

